Subject: [M152] Fix RFH state checks in EvictBFCachedClientsIfLastActive

Original change's description:
> Fix RFH state checks in EvictBFCachedClientsIfLastActive
>
> SharedWorkerHost::EvictBFCachedClientsIfLastActive checks if the main
> frame currently navigating away (render_frame_host) is the last active
> client of a SharedWorker. If so, it evicts other BFCached clients of
> the worker and returns true so that render_frame_host is blocked from
> entering BFCache.
>
> Previously, EvictBFCachedClientsIfLastActive checked
> `if (other_rfh->IsActive()) return false;`. For inactive RFHs that
> are not in BFCache (such as frames in kPendingDeletion or running
> unload handlers), IsActive() returned false, causing them to be added
> to `bf_cached_clients`. This resulted in calling
> EvictFromBackForwardCacheWithReason on RFHs that were not in
> BFCache, triggering a crash.
>
> Changing the check to `if (!other_rfh->IsInBackForwardCache()) return false;` was also insufficient because it treated inactive
> non-BFCached RFHs (e.g., kPendingDeletion) as active clients,
> returning false early and incorrectly allowing render_frame_host to
> enter BFCache when no active clients remained.
>
> This CL explicitly handles all three RFH lifecycle categories:
> 1. Active (other_rfh->IsActive()): Returns false because another
>    active client exists.
> 2. In BFCache (other_rfh->IsInBackForwardCache()): Pushed to
>    bf_cached_clients to be evicted if render_frame_host is the last
>    active client.
> 3. Inactive & not in BFCache (kPendingDeletion, etc.): Skipped, as it
>    is neither active nor stored in BFCache.
>
